# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for craudio_test on Linux, under the -tsan
# presets (set on the test in CMakeLists.txt). Third-party code only, each
# with its reason; Carrottus's own reports are fixed, never listed here.

# ALSA's pipewire PCM plugin takes PipeWire's thread-loop lock (libspa's
# loop_lock) and alsa-lib's PCM lock in both orders inside libasound:
# snd_pcm_sw_params_current under the loop lock, snd_pcm_drop's loop lock
# under the PCM lock. Both locks and both orders are inside the plugin, on
# the one thread Carrottus Audio calls from; nothing Carrottus Audio does orders them.
# Seen 2026-10-25 on Fedora 44, PipeWire 1.6.9, alsa-lib 1.2.16.
deadlock:loop_lock
# The same report as named by a symbolizer that can't read the library's
# MiniDebugInfo (clang's runtime through addr2line, 2026-11-04): loop_lock
# reads "<null>" in libspa-support.so, so the entry names the module, whose
# locks are all PipeWire's.
deadlock:libspa-support.so

# ALSA's ioplug ring buffer, under the pipewire PCM plugin: the buffer is
# alsa-lib's own (malloc'd by snd_pcm_mmap inside snd_pcm_hw_params, the
# stream being RW access, not mmap). snd_pcm_writei copies the caller's
# frames into it under the PCM lock on Carrottus Audio's audio thread
# (snd_pcm_area_copy <- alsa_transfer <- alsa_audio_thread), and PipeWire's
# data-loop thread copies them out under its own loop lock
# (snd_pcm_area_copy, no frame of ours). The two hand slots over through
# the plugin's hw_ptr/appl_ptr in uninstrumented code, so ThreadSanitizer
# sees no order between a slot read and its reuse a lap later. Carrottus Audio's
# own buffers (scratch, planes) are only the source of the first copy, on
# its one thread. Seen 2026-11-04 on Fedora 44, PipeWire 1.6.2,
# alsa-lib 1.2.16.1 (ThinkPad P1).
race:snd_pcm_area_copy
